WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:12.640
TELL ME ONE THING. Do you feel sad?
Or maybe lonely. Or depressed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.680
Anxious. Tired. Burnt out.&nbsp;
Full of dread, or despair.&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:27.520
Maybe you feel alienated from&nbsp;
other people; apart, adrift.&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:38.320
If so, you’re not alone. Everyone feels the same&nbsp;
way. They are the dominant feelings of our time.&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.920
You’ve probably heard this described&nbsp;
as a quote “mental health crisis.”&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.920
And that is true. Something has gone wrong&nbsp;
with our heads, with our “mental health.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.720
Something is out of whack.
Just take a look at the world around us.&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.440
We live in an age of overwhelming atomization.
More and more people report that they feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:06.160
deeply lonely: Americans, especially young&nbsp;
Americans, report having fewer friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.080
and fewer romantic partners or meaningful&nbsp;
connections than people of past generations.&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:19.520
Perhaps not coincidentally, rates of depression&nbsp;
and anxiety, of behaviors associated with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:27.360
are going up. WAY up. More people than ever are&nbsp;
using antidepressant medications, like SSRIs.&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:37.040
And more and more people are committing&nbsp;
suicide, or considering it. In June 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.200
a quarter of young Americans seriously&nbsp;
considered committing suicide.&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.760
Suicides and drug overdoses,&nbsp;
classified as “deaths of despair,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:57.600
have become so common that they’ve driven down&nbsp;
American life expectancy from its peak in 2014.&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:08.960
By 2021, fentanyl overdose was the MOST COMMON&nbsp;
form of death among Americans aged 18 to 45.&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.880
All around us we detect a mood of&nbsp;
pessimism, nihilism, self-destruction.&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.840
Fewer people have children;&nbsp;
more people kill themselves;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:29.200
more and more people die from overdoses; more and&nbsp;
more people become dependent on pharmaceuticals.&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.840
Talk to young people, or students, or really&nbsp;
ANYONE and it’s hard to deny the simple truth:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.920
something is very wrong.&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.840
But the response to this “mental&nbsp;
health crisis” is a very telling one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.920
Our institutions – our schools, our&nbsp;
colleges, our workplaces: what do they do?&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:04.560
They think it’s as simple as “work-life balance.”
They encourage you to take regular five-minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.840
breaks from your work.
They give you Zoloft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.120
and might suggest that you up your subscription.
They tell you to install a push notification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.840
on your phone, to let you know if you’ve&nbsp;
spent too much time staring at your screen.&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.640
They do everything EXCEPT one&nbsp;
thing: solve the essential problem.&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:33.360
They will keep you complacent and&nbsp;
productive enough for their purposes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:40.400
but the problem – the depression, the anxiety,&nbsp;
the loneliness: that will not go away.&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.840
Because they treat this as an INDIVIDUAL&nbsp;
problem, and not a SOCIAL problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.520
They turn a social problem, a political problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.360
into a BIOLOGICAL one.
And while that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.480
make sense on an individual level, it doesn’t&nbsp;
make sense from the perspective of a society.&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.920
Because it is NOT NORMAL for so&nbsp;
many people to be this depressed.&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:14.160
It is not normal for so many people, especially&nbsp;
young people, to be so reliant on antidepressants.&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.640
It is not normal for them to feel so alone,&nbsp;
for them to kill themselves at such high rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.040
for them to report such high rates of&nbsp;
anxiety and loneliness and depression.&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.600
THAT IS NOT NORMAL.
Because there really IS something very wrong.&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.720
Mental illness, depression, anxiety&nbsp;
– these may manifest neurologically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.680
but they are SOCIALLY and SPIRITUALLY rooted.
If so many people in a society are depressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:51.920
if so many people are killing themselves, if&nbsp;
so many are reliant on pills trying to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:05:00.000
suicide – that says something, something&nbsp;
bad, about the society in question.&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.480
Because something is broken about our society.
We are the loneliest people in human history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:14.000
There has never been a society as ATOMIZED,&nbsp;
as ALIENATED, as ALONE as we are now.&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.720
It is unprecedented in the hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
of years that humans have been on earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:29.440
for us to be this separated from each other.
Because the truth is that, at a basic level, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:40.480
need OTHER PEOPLE. We find ourselves, our&nbsp;
identities, our meanings, through other people.&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:51.760
We are social animals. We have an urge to love,&nbsp;
to be loved, to be recognized, to be respected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:06:01.680
to BELONG. We need other people. We need&nbsp;
family; we need friends; we need a COMMUNITY.&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:10.800
And we live in a society now&nbsp;
where that is impossible.&nbsp;

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:18.960
Let’s begin with a man named Robert Putnam.
A few decades ago, Putnam was a famous professor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.880
a sociologist who studied the&nbsp;
trends and changes in American life.&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:29.520
And in the 1990s, Putnam was taking a look at&nbsp;
what had shifted in America since the 1960s.&nbsp;

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:38.000
And as he looked, Putnam began to find something&nbsp;
disturbing: American community was falling apart.&nbsp;

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.960
If you look back at American history,&nbsp;
you find something kind of incredible.&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.920
The level of organic community&nbsp;
in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:55.120
from its creation to just a few&nbsp;
decades ago, was REMARKABLY strong.&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.960
Go back a hundred years, and look&nbsp;
at the communities around you.&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.440
Towns and cities in America used to be places&nbsp;
with a VIBRANT and THRIVING communal life.&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:12.640
They had labor unions, charity&nbsp;
associations, gardening clubs, bridge clubs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.680
churches, bowling leagues, scout troops.
People went to local parades;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.840
they knew their neighbors. They knew&nbsp;
what was going on in their community.&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.400
And that sense of COMMUNITY is&nbsp;
what gave MEANING to their lives.&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.760
It’s how they met their friends, their&nbsp;
spouses. It’s where they raised their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.800
kids. It’s what filled out the contours&nbsp;
of their lives, what made them happy.&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.960
You could be any ordinary person, living an&nbsp;
ordinary life, and you could find dignity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:49.200
community, a sense of WORTH, in these places.
You had a social world all around you;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.720
you had a net of support all around you.
But by the nineties, as Putnam was looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.640
at the country around him, he&nbsp;
found a social world in decay.&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.000
Because starting in the nineteen&nbsp;
sixties, accelerating ever since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.880
then – all these communities and&nbsp;
institutions had been collapsing.&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:18.000
Church attendance was falling; local clubs&nbsp;
were closing down. Local newspapers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.520
losing readers and being shut down. Unions were&nbsp;
losing members. Families were falling apart.&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:31.360
Community was disintegrating. Americans&nbsp;
were WITHDRAWING, going inwards.&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.880
They stopped knowing who their neighbors&nbsp;
were. They stopped going to parades or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:41.120
civic events. They stopped being a part&nbsp;
of any real local community at all.&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.440
Even things as simple as local bowling&nbsp;
leagues were disappearing. Putnam wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:54.960
that Americans had begun, quote, “BOWLING ALONE.”
Americans were now spending more and more time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:02.000
without other people. They were expressing&nbsp;
lower and lower levels of social trust – not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:09.440
just in their leaders, but in EACH OTHER.
The social fabric of American life was unraveling.&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:18.800
And once you understand that basic fact&nbsp;
– the decline of organic communities –&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:23.440
the last few decades of American&nbsp;
life start to make a lot of sense.&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:29.040
Because without organic communities around&nbsp;
them, people were deprived of the support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:35.840
and comfort and stability they had once&nbsp;
enjoyed, not to mention friendship and love.&nbsp;

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.520
They regarded each other with more suspicion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.120
Life became more zero-sum.&nbsp;
Everyone was in it for themselves.&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:54.560
When everyone around you is a stranger, life just&nbsp;
becomes about you. About getting what’s yours.&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:04.080
About becoming a CEO or a billionaire, getting out&nbsp;
on top, screwing over everyone else if necessary.&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.720
All of life is consumed by&nbsp;
the logic of the market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:16.160
Everything becomes a marketplace; everything can&nbsp;
be bought and sold; working, earning, competing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:23.200
consuming – that becomes the sum total of life.&nbsp;
Money became more important than life itself.&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:30.880
And what did you get for it?
Life did not get better. It got worse.&nbsp;

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:35.120
You worked longer hours for lower wages.
You paid more than your parents or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:40.560
grandparents did to go to school or see&nbsp;
a doctor; owning your own home, raising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:46.480
children – these became more and more difficult.&nbsp;
It got harder just to keep your head above water.&nbsp;

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:51.600
And you would do it without the support that&nbsp;
your parents or grandparents had enjoyed:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:55.520
fewer friends, fewer&nbsp;
relationships, less community.&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:06.640
Life became colder, emptier, more unforgiving.&nbsp;
More people lived without human companionship.&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:14.000
Even the most successful people were unhappy. They&nbsp;
reported high rates of depression, of anxiety;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:19.760
even THEY were frustrated, and lonely.
And young people, just coming into the world:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.720
THEY have been the worst off. Without organic&nbsp;
communities, everyone becomes the same:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:35.120
identity becomes a matter of consumer preference.
In the age of the Internet, atomized people drift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:42.560
into alternate realities, where they can be&nbsp;
anyone but themselves: cults, extremism, scams.&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:50.240
Amid the vast meaningless of a society&nbsp;
in decay, they forge new identities.&nbsp;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.480
At first it was young people;&nbsp;
then it was everyone else.&nbsp;

00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:00.400
We spent more time with electronics than we&nbsp;
did with humans. Our identities were shaped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:05.200
by algorithms written by corporations, in&nbsp;
order to make us buy stuff we don’t need.&nbsp;

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.520
Social media – designed to make you feel&nbsp;
angry and alone, but also to addict you:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:18.880
THAT became the core of our social existence.
ASMR videos that simulate intimacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:26.240
internet porn addiction that makes real&nbsp;
romance impossible, video streams of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:31.840
playing video games and talking: these are&nbsp;
all substitutes for the human companionship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.920
that is lacking everywhere else in our society.
But the internet didn’t fix it. It only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:40.800
accelerated the atomization,&nbsp;
the loneliness, the depression.&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:48.160
Older people, once treated as sources of guidance&nbsp;
and wisdom, began to be viewed as useless, wastes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:53.680
of space; they are shuffled into nursing homes.
High rates of elder loneliness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:13:05.840
has led to high rates of elder suicide.
This is the world that we created: cold and empty.&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:10.880
And it’s no wonder why more people&nbsp;
became dependent on antidepressant drugs;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:20.640
why more died from overdoses; why more expressed&nbsp;
a desire to kill themselves, to not exist at all.&nbsp;

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.480
These are what we call “morbid symptoms.”
These are reflections of a society in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:32.240
which the market has taken over all of&nbsp;
life, in which human community and human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:36.320
values have all but disappeared.
Traditional forms of community and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:40.960
solidarity that have given meaning&nbsp;
to human life have been dismantled.&nbsp;

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:48.160
Life has become about lines on a graph&nbsp;
going up and down. Human community has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:57.360
not only been neglected, but actively destroyed.
All of human life has undergone an impoverishment.&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:04.400
We live in a society that is structurally unable&nbsp;
to meet our social needs: the need for community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:08.080
for love, for being with other people.&nbsp;

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:12.560
It is a society that whittles&nbsp;
your humanity down to statistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:18.960
Stock market lines tick up; so do death tolls, and&nbsp;
suicides, and overdoses. The abstraction of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:26.240
becomes more real than our actual lives.
It becomes a society without meaning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.360
A society that wants you to be&nbsp;
constantly working, constantly consuming.&nbsp;

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:37.360
It does not want you to talk to other people; it&nbsp;
does not want you to be a part of a community;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:40.400
it does not want you to find&nbsp;
meaning outside the market.&nbsp;

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.840
A society that wants you&nbsp;
to be an atomized consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:51.200
so that it can manipulate you. It wants you to&nbsp;
be an atomized worker so that it can exploit you.&nbsp;

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:02.400
The reason you feel this way is because you want&nbsp;
to live a real life. You want to be with other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:12.960
people, to join hands with them, to recognize&nbsp;
them as a friend, a partner, a child, a person.&nbsp;

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:19.440
And no matter how materially satisfying any&nbsp;
society is, we are YEARNING for something:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:29.760
something that CAN exist, that SHOULD exist, but&nbsp;
that our current system cannot ALLOW to exist.&nbsp;

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:36.640
We are yearning for community. We are&nbsp;
yearning for other people, for connection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:41.920
for friendship. We are yearning for&nbsp;
a world where those things can exist.&nbsp;

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:49.840
And that world can exist.
In small glimpses, in little moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:55.440
it already DOES exist. It is a&nbsp;
world that is struggling to be born.&nbsp;

00:15:56.080 --> 00:16:01.440
There is nothing impossible about that world:&nbsp;
because the ultimate truth about the world is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:07.840
that it is something that WE CAN imagine,&nbsp;
that WE CAN create: a more beautiful world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:21.840
a more wonderful world, a more human world.
I’m Marianne Williamson, for the Gravel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:51.440
Institute.

